$$\n\nIn our case, we need to check if the following equation is exact:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{{x}^{3}}(x\\,\\text{d}y-y\\,\\text{d}x) & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nFor our transformed equation, we have  \n\n$$\nP(x,y)=-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{3}},\\,Q(x,y)=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{3}})=-\\frac{2y}{{x}^{3}} \\\\ \\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}})=-\\frac{2y}{{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x},\n$$\n\nthe transformed equation $\\text{is}$ exact.\n\nTherefore, $I(x,y)=\\frac{y}{{x}^{3}}$ $\\text{is}$ an integrating factor for the initial differential equation." 13194139535339 true] [17592186344493 1000 #edb/ref 17592186344494 13194139535339 true] [17592186344493 1000 #edb/ref 17592186344495 13194139535339 true] [17592186344493 1000 #edb/ref 17592186344496 13194139535339 true] [17592186344493 1000 #edb/ref 17592186344497 13194139535339 true] [17592186344493 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045451 13194139535339 true] [17592186344493 1041 "field-1" 13194139535339 true] [17592186344493 1104 #edb/ref 17592186344497 13194139535339 true] [17592186344493 1110 #uuid "4e3c39b7-fecf-5ea0-8367-f34a03bcccc8" 13194139535339 true] [17592186344494 1034 "\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}y" 13194139535339 true] [17592186344494 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535339 true] [17592186344494 1111 #uuid "036df0a1-b413-53c0-bbe7-5bbb1a2c4f11" 13194139535339 true] [17592186344495 1034 "-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}\\,\\text{d}y" 13194139535339 true] [17592186344495 1103 #edb/ref 1759218604