re $g(x)$ is independent of $y,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(x)=\\operatorname{exp}⁡(\\int g(x)\\,\\text{d}x)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\operatorname{exp}⁡(x)$ denotes the exponential function ${e}^{x}.$\n\nFirst, we rewrite our equation in differential form: \n\n$$\n-(4xy+3x)\\,\\text{d}x+\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nLet's denote \n\n$$\nP(x,y)=-(4xy+3x),\\,Q(x,y)=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-(4xy+3x))=-4x \\\\ \\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}) & =\\frac{1}{1}(-4x-0) \\\\  & =-4x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis a function of $x$ only, we can conclude that an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & =\\operatorname{exp}⁡(\\int -4x\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{exp}⁡(-2{x}^{2}) \\\\  & ={e}^{-2{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand by multiplying our differential equation by the integrating factor, we have\n\n$$\nI(x)\\cdot (-(4xy+3x)\\,\\text{d}x+\\text{d}y)=I(x)\\cdot 0\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n{e}^{-2{x}^{2}}\\cdot (-(4xy+3x)\\,\\text{d}x+\\text{d}y)=0.\n$$" 13194139535339 true] [17592186344518 1000 #edb/ref 17592186344519 13194139535339 true] [17592186344518 1000 #edb/ref 17592186344520 13194139535339 true] [17592186344518 1000 #edb/ref 17592186344521 13194139535339 true] [17592186344518 1000 #edb/ref 17592186344522 13194139535339 true] [17592186344518 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045451 13194139535339 true] [17592186344518 1041 "field-1" 13194139535339 true] [17592186344518 1104 #edb/ref 17592186344520 13194139535339 true] [17592186344518 1110 #uuid "388368d0-086a-5c67-b7e8-993b26f4f89e" 13194139535339 true] [17592186344519 1034 "\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})" 13194139535339 true] [17592186344519 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535339 true] [17592186344519 1111 #uuid "309ee086-ad04-5a47-85c1-faa9cfc0cfa4" 13194139535339 true] [17592186