9 true] [17592186344558 1145 "A function $I(x,y)$ is an integrating factor for the differential equation\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nif the following equation is exact:\n\n$$\nI(x,y)[P(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y]=0.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we need to check if the following equation is exact:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{y}({x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x) & =0 \\\\ -y\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nFor our transformed equation, we have  \n\n$$\nP(x,y)=-y,\\,Q(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-y)=-1 \\\\ \\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y})=\\frac{2x}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}\\ne \\frac{∂Q}{∂x},\n$$\n\nthe transformed equation $\\text{isn't}$ exact.\n\nTherefore, $I(y)=\\frac{1}{y}$ $\\text{isn't}$ an integrating factor for the initial differential equation." 13194139535339 true] [17592186344559 1000 #edb/ref 17592186344560 13194139535339 true] [17592186344559 1000 #edb/ref 17592186344561 13194139535339 true] [17592186344559 1000 #edb/ref 17592186344562 13194139535339 true] [17592186344559 1000 #edb/ref 17592186344563 13194139535339 true] [17592186344559 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045451 13194139535339 true] [17592186344559 1041 "field-1" 13194139535339 true] [17592186344559 1104 #edb/ref 17592186344561 13194139535339 true] [17592186344559 1110 #uuid "d8000a6d-c0f5-535a-9063-218c386a95b1" 13194139535339 true] [17592186344560 1034 "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}\\,\\text{d}x-y\\,\\text{d}y" 13194139535339 true] [17592186344560 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535339 true] [17592186344560 1111 #uuid "39709273-a80c-5f33-8182-4732fff32276" 13194139535339 true] [175921