edb/ref 17592186344589 13194139535339 true] [17592186344583 1017 #edb/ref 17592186344594 13194139535339 true] [17592186344583 1017 #edb/ref 17592186344599 13194139535339 true] [17592186344583 1017 #edb/ref 17592186344604 13194139535339 true] [17592186344583 1017 #edb/ref 17592186344609 13194139535339 true] [17592186344583 1017 #edb/ref 17592186344614 13194139535339 true] [17592186344583 1017 #edb/ref 17592186344619 13194139535339 true] [17592186344583 1089 "q-337523" 13194139535339 true] [17592186344583 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535339 true] [17592186344583 1100 "Suppose we would like to find an integrating factor to convert the differential equation\n\n$$\n3x\\,{y}^{′}=-6y-4{x}^{2}\n$$\n\ninto an exact equation using an integrating factor. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}=g(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is independent of $y,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(x)={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\operatorname{exp}⁡(x)$ denotes the exponential function ${e}^{x}.$\n\nFirst, we rewrite our equation in differential form. This gives\n\n$$\n{{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nLet's denote\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=4{x}^{2}+6y,\\,Q(x,y)=3x.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\,{{field-4}}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\,{{field-5}}\n$$\n\nSince for this equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})={{field-6}},\n$$\n\nwhich depends on $x$ only, then an integrating factor for our equation is\n\n$$\nI(x)={{field-7}},\n$$\n\nand we arrive at the exact equation\n\n$$\n{{field-8}}.\n$$" 13194139535339 true] [17592186344583 1113 #uuid "89937b0f-baea-5172-ac5f-17e646ff4d1d" 13194139535339 true] [17592186344583 1145 "For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=g(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is independent of $y,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(x)=\\operatorname{exp}⁡(\\int g(x)\\,\\text{d}x)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\operatorname{exp}⁡(x)$ denotes the exponential function ${e}^{x}.$\n\nFirst, we rewrite our equation in differential form: \n\n$$\n(4{x}^{2}+6y)\\,\\text{d}x+3x\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nLet's denote \n\n$$\nP(x,y)=4{x}^{2}+6y,\\,Q(x,y)=3x.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4{x}^{2}+6y)=6 \\\\ \\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}3x=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}) & =\\frac{1}{3x}(6-3) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3x}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis a function of $x$ only, we can conclude that an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & =\\operatorname{exp}⁡(\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{exp}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\  & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand by multiplying our differential equation by the integrating factor, we have\n\n$$\nI(x)\\cdot ((4{x}^{2}+6y)\\,\\text{d}x+3x\\,\\text{d}y)=I(x)\\cdot 0\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\nx\\cdot ((4{x}^{2}+6y)\\,\\text{d}x+3x\\,\\text{d}y)=0.\n$$" 13194139535339 true] [17592186344584 1000 #edb/ref 17592186344585 13194139535339 true] [17592186344584 1000 #edb/ref 17592186344586 13194139535339 true] [17592186344584 1000 #edb/ref 17592186344587 13194139535339 true] [17592186344584 1000 #edb/ref 17592186344588 13194139535339 true] [17592186344584 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045451 13194139535339 true] [17592186344584 1041 "field-1" 13194139535339 true] [17592186344584 1104 #edb/ref 17592186344586 13194139535339 true] [17592186344584 1110 #uuid "3e324ef6-6527-5246-a71f-0cbbce07971a" 13194139535339 true] [17592186344585 1034 "\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})" 13194139535339 true] [17592186344585 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535339 true] [17592186344585 1111 #uuid "208d47cd-7