\\,{{field-3}}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\,{{field-4}}\n$$\n\nSince for this equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})={{field-5}},\n$$\n\nwhich depends on $y$ only, then an integrating factor for our equation is\n\n$$\nI(y)={{field-6}}.\n$$\n\nand we arrive at the exact equation\n\n$$\n{{field-7}}.\n$$" 13194139535339 true] [17592186344624 1113 #uuid "2468ae6a-5d7a-552b-a271-4a092bb2d9ad" 13194139535339 true] [17592186344624 1145 "For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=h(y)\n$$\n\nwhere $h(y)$ is independent of $x,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(y)=\\operatorname{exp}⁡(-\\int h(y)\\,\\text{d}y).\n$$\n\nLet's denote\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=2{y}^{3},\\,Q(x,y)=-xy.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{y}^{3})=6{y}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-xy)=-y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}) & =\\frac{1}{2{y}^{3}}(6{y}^{2}-(-y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2{y}^{3}}(6{y}^{2}+y) \\\\  & =\\frac{6y+1}{2{y}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis a function of $y$ only, we can conclude that an integrating factor for our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(y) & =\\operatorname{exp}⁡(\\int -\\frac{6y+1}{2{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{exp}⁡(-(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-\\frac{1}{2y})) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{exp}⁡(-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)\\cdot \\operatorname{exp}⁡(\\frac{1}{2y}) \\\\  & ={y}^{-3}{e}^{1/(2y)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand by multiplying our differential equation by the integrating factor, we have\n\n$$\nI(y)\\cdot (2{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x-xy\\,\\text{d}y)=0\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n{y}^{-3}{e}^{1/(2y)}(2{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x-xy\\,\\text{d}y)=0.\n$$" 13194139535339 true] [17592186344625 1000 #edb/ref 17592186344626 13194139535339 true] [17592186344625 1000 #edb/ref 1759218