b07-fdb7-5aef-b834-e70a6b70d9ed" 13194139535339 true] [17592186344654 1000 #edb/ref 17592186344655 13194139535339 true] [17592186344654 1000 #edb/ref 17592186344656 13194139535339 true] [17592186344654 1000 #edb/ref 17592186344657 13194139535339 true] [17592186344654 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045451 13194139535339 true] [17592186344654 1041 "field-7" 13194139535339 true] [17592186344654 1104 #edb/ref 17592186344655 13194139535339 true] [17592186344654 1110 #uuid "fb1ccea1-086c-5320-b04a-c69042c4e25b" 13194139535339 true] [17592186344655 1034 "{y}^{-3}{e}^{1/(2y)}\\cdot (2{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x-xy\\,\\text{d}y)=0" 13194139535339 true] [17592186344655 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535339 true] [17592186344655 1111 #uuid "72b9f9c5-2e07-5d97-8e23-bb1b9bf6d8e8" 13194139535339 true] [17592186344656 1034 "{y}^{-2}{e}^{1/(2y)}\\cdot (2{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x-xy\\,\\text{d}y)=0" 13194139535339 true] [17592186344656 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535339 true] [17592186344656 1111 #uuid "79bef994-19aa-530a-b936-c850a96b87d0" 13194139535339 true] [17592186344657 1034 "{y}^{-3}{e}^{-1/(2y)}\\cdot (2{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x-xy\\,\\text{d}y)=0" 13194139535339 true] [17592186344657 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535339 true] [17592186344657 1111 #uuid "b47cb404-1f29-5aa1-99bd-feac66346c54" 13194139535339 true] [17592186344658 1017 #edb/ref 17592186344659 13194139535339 true] [17592186344658 1017 #edb/ref 17592186344664 13194139535339 true] [17592186344658 1089 "q-337637" 13194139535339 true] [17592186344658 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535339 true] [17592186344658 1100 "Consider the inexact differential equation\n\n$$\n(\\frac{5{y}^{2}}{x}+2{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x+(5y+2x{y}^{2}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})\\,\\text{d}y=0.\n$$\n\nIt is known that \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=\\frac{2}{x}\n$$\n\nwhere the functions $P$ and $Q$ are given by \n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\frac{5{y}^{2}}{x}+2{y}^{3},\\,Q(x,y)=5y+2x{y}^{2}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nUse this information to find a suitable integrating factor $I(x)$ and hence find the general solution to the equation.$\\,$ A suitable integrating factor is $I(x)=$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The general solution is {{field-2}}, where $c$ is a constant." 13194139535339 true] [17592186344658 1113 #uuid "17762e70-5ac2-5a8b-a38d-88dab34b7f4a" 13194139535339 true] [17592186344658 1145 "For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=g(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is independent of $y,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(x)=\\operatorname{exp}⁡(\\int g(x)\\,\\text{d}x)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\operatorname{exp}⁡(x)$ denotes the exponential function ${e}^{x}.$\n\nWe are given that \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=\\frac{2}{x}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{2}{x}$ is a function of $x$ only, we can conclude that the integrating factor for the equation is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int 2{x}^{-1}\\text{ }\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our equation by the integrating factor, we arrive at the following exact differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}[(\\frac{5{y}^{2}}{x}+2{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x+(5y+2x{y}^{2}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})\\,\\text{d}y] & =0 \\\\ {{(5x{y}^{2}+2{x}^{2}{y}^{3})}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{P}}\\,\\text{d}x+{{(5{x}^{2}y+2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+2)}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{Q}}\\,\\text{d}y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor this exact equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{P}(x,y)=5x{y}^{2}+2{x}^{2}{y}^{3},\\,\\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)=5{x}^{2}y+2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+2.\n$$\n\nTo solve the equation, let's first integrate $\\overset{~}{P}(x,y)$ with respect to $x.$\nThe result should include an arbitrary function of $y,$ which we'll call $F(y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int \\overset{~}{P}(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+F(y) \\\\  & =\\int (5x{y}^{2}+2{x}^{2}{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x+F(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}{y}^{3}+F(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, let's integrate $\\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)$ with respect to $y.$\nThe result should include an arbitrary function of $x,$ which we'll call $G(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int \\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+G(x) \\\\  & =\\int (5{x}^{2}y+2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+2)\\,\\text{d}y+G(x) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}{y}^{3}+2y+G(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two expressions, we must have\n\n$$\nF(y)=2y,\\,G(x)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y)=c$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}{y}^{3}+2y=c.\n$$" 13194139535339 true] [17592186344659 1000 #edb/ref 17592186344660 13194139535339 true] [17592186344659 1000 #edb/ref 17592186344661 13194139535339 true] [17592186344659 1000 #edb/ref 17592186344662 13194139535339 true] [17592186344659 1000 #edb/ref 17592186344663 13194139535339 true] [17592186344659 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045451 13194139535339 true] [17592186344659 1041 "field-1" 13194139535339 true] [17592186344659 1104 #edb/ref 17592186344663 13194139535339 true] [17592186344659 1110 #uuid "55c1fbdb-01e9-594d-a2e9-b08eba986409" 13194139535339 true] [17592186344660 1034 "\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}" 13194139535339 true] [17592186344660 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535339 true] [17592186344660 1111 #uuid "f81643fb-31f9-5735-8eea-48718cca96eb" 13194139535339 true] [17592186344661 1034 "{e}^{2x}" 13194139535339 true] [17592186344661 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535339 true] [17592186344661 1111 #uuid "b343c865-e53f-5df8-a2dc-7d8b35613d86" 13194139535339 true] [17592186344662 1034 "{x}^{3}" 13194139535339 true] [17592186344662 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535339 true] [17592186344662 1111 #uuid "6b48dcfa-c5cb-50d7-8901-6b233e81328c" 13194139535339 true] [17592186344663 1034 "{x}^{2}" 13194139535339 true] [17592186344663 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535339 true] [17592186344663 1111 #uuid "e54180f2-51cd-5305-809d-1807e5871b82