\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=x({y}^{3}+1),\\,Q(x,y)={y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x({y}^{3}+1))=3x{y}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2})=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}) & =\\frac{1}{x({y}^{3}+1)}(3x{y}^{2}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{{y}^{3}+1},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis a function of $y$ only, we can conclude that an integrating factor for our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(y) & =\\operatorname{exp}⁡(-\\int \\frac{3{y}^{2}}{{y}^{3}+1}\\,\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{exp}⁡(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{3}+1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{y}^{3}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand by multiplying our differential equation by the integrating factor, we have\n\n$$\nI(y)\\cdot (x({y}^{3}+1)\\,\\text{d}x+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y)=I(y)\\cdot 0\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{y}^{3}+1}(x({y}^{3}+1)\\,\\text{d}x+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y)=0.\n$$" 13194139535339 true] [17592186344670 1000 #edb/ref 17592186344671 13194139535339 true] [17592186344670 1000 #edb/ref 17592186344672 13194139535339 true] [17592186344670 1000 #edb/ref 17592186344673 13194139535339 true] [17592186344670 1000 #edb/ref 17592186344674 13194139535339 true] [17592186344670 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045451 13194139535339 true] [17592186344670 1041 "field-1" 13194139535339 true] [17592186344670 1104 #edb/ref 17592186344673 13194139535339 true] [17592186344670 1110 #uuid "dcc694c9-8780-5024-bd8f-f6a50d6b437a" 13194139535339 true] [17592186344671 1034 "\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})" 13194139535339 true] [17592186344671 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535339 true] [17592186344671 1111 #uuid "eece721c-eee3-5e71-9926-341d220a1315" 13194139535339 true] [17592186344672 1034 "\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})" 13194139535339 true] [17592186344672 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535339 true] [17592186344672 1111 #uuid "526a00c6-8671-5261-a2a5-0f