n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x,y) & ={e}^{-\\int 3/y\\text{ }\\text{d}y} \\\\  & ={e}^{-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡y} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{y}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our equation by the integrating factor, we arrive at the following exact differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{y}^{3}}(yx\\,\\text{d}x-({x}^{2}+5{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}y) & =0 \\\\ {{(\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}})}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{P}}\\,\\text{d}x+{{(-1)(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{3}}+5)}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{Q}}\\,\\text{d}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor this exact equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{P}(x,y)=\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}},\\,\\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)=-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{3}}-5.\n$$\n\nTo solve the equation, let's first integrate $\\overset{~}{P}(x,y)$ with respect to $x.$\nThe result should include an arbitrary function of $y,$ which we'll call $F(y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int \\overset{~}{P}(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+F(y) \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+F(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2{y}^{2}}+F(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, let's integrate $\\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)$ with respect to $y.$\nThe result should include an arbitrary function of $x,$ which we'll call $G(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int \\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+G(x) \\\\  & =\\int (-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{3}}-5)\\,\\text{d}y+G(x) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2{y}^{2}}-5y+G(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we see that we must have\n\n$$\nF(y)=-5y,\\,G(x)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y)=c$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2{y}^{2}}-5y=c.\n$$" 13194139535339 true] [17592186344722 1000 #edb/ref 17592186344723 13194139535339 true] [17592186344722 1000 #edb/ref 17592186344724 13194139535339 true] [17592186344722 1000 #edb/ref 17592186344725 13194139535339 true] [17592186344722 1000 #edb/ref 17592186344726 13194139535339 true] [17592186344722 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045451 13194139535339 true] [17592186344722 1041 "field-1" 13194139535339 true] [17592186344722 1104 #edb/ref 1759218634